# hilavitkutin-frozen-implementors, Design Overview

These are implementors as a consumer would write them, from the contracts alone, kept frozen so that a later change to the contracts or the plumbing shows up as a change they would need. A frozen file is edited before its form locks only in a commit that updates its ledger hash, raises its breaks count and adds a line to `BREAKS.toml`; after the lock it is not edited, and a changed form arrives as new files beside the old. The repo lint `frozen-ledger` holds the ledger over every file of the crate, its manifest included.

`#![no_std]`, no alloc, no `unsafe`, no `#![feature]`, and `publish = false`. Depends on `hilavitkutin-contracts` alone, and the lint refuses any other dependency, dev and build dependencies included. Neither store compares formats: the bind did, once.

## The stores

`Columns` keeps `COLUMNS` stored columns of up to `CAP` records, each column one contiguous run of cells, the record at position `at` in slot `at`, committed and staged as two whole tables that trade places on a swap. `Records` keeps its committed cells record by record, each record's cells side by side, the record at position `at` in slot `extent - 1 - at`; its staged cells are one run per column, last key first, and its swap copies the columns a step wrote into the records. Both keep a table of the shape's keys in key order, `Keys`, and answer a neighbour read by walking the domain's `reach` and halving that table. `ColumnsRead` and `RecordsRead` are their committed views.

Both hand out their staged side as `Lanes`, one slice per stored column over a run of positions, and their staged parts are `ColumnsWrite` and `RecordsWrite`, which are `Lanes` in two orders. An `Order` says where a position's cell sits in a run and how a run is cut at a position: `Ahead` puts a position at its offset from the run's first and keeps the cells below a cut first, `Behind` puts it at its offset from the run's last and keeps them second. `Lanes::take` moves the columns a unit writes into a part of their own and marks them written; `Lanes::split` cuts every column the part holds at one position; a part writes only where it holds the column and the position. On a swap each store commits the columns taken since the last one and carries the rest across: `Columns` by copying an untaken column's committed cells into its staged table before the two trade places, `Records` by copying only the taken columns into its records.

Each has an inherent `poison_staged`, filling the staged storage with one word, which is how frame isolation is tested; `set` and `cell` load and read committed cells by position and `ColumnNo` for tests. `value` reads a value back from a word.

## The visiting orders

`Forward` visits every position in key order. `Permuted` visits position `(a * i + b) mod extent` at step `i`, `a` coprime with the extent and both drawn from its seed. Both cut the sequence into morsels of a `Morsel`'s size, the last short, and an extent of zero visits nothing.
